  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🐊 Lizardfolk Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Hissing Draconic names and plain descriptive ones, earned by deed or trait."
    },
    "intro": "Lizardfolk see the world in practical terms, and their names do too. In D&D's <em>Volo's Guide to Monsters</em>, lizardfolk take their names from Draconic, and the name is a simple description given by the tribe for something the lizardfolk did or is known for. This **lizardfolk name generator** gives you hissing Draconic-style names, plain descriptive names in Common, or both together.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How lizardfolk get their names",
        "content": "<p>A lizardfolk's name isn't chosen for how it sounds. It's a description, earned through a deed or a trait the tribe has noticed. A lizardfolk might be renamed if they do something more notable later.</p><p>Because the names are Draconic words, outsiders often only hear the sound. Ask a lizardfolk what their name means and you'll get a plain answer: \"Swims-Deep\", \"Stormeye\", \"Patient-Hunter\". The <strong>Both</strong> format gives you the two side by side.</p><p>The Draconic-style names in this generator are invented to sound right, not real Draconic words, so they don't have fixed meanings. Pair one with a descriptive name to decide what it means for your character.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "What the names sound like",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Hissing and rolling sounds:</strong> ss, sk, rr and th. Kressk, Ussarth, Zhurrk.</li><li><strong>Short:</strong> one or two syllables, said with a flick of the tongue.</li><li><strong>Descriptive names are blunt.</strong> They describe the body (Green-Scale), a skill (Eel-Catcher) or a habit (Sun-Basker). Lizardfolk don't flatter.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Playing a lizardfolk",
        "content": "<p>Lizardfolk player characters are fun because they think so differently from the rest of the party. The name can show that:</p><ul><li><strong>Ask for the party's names in the same style.</strong> A lizardfolk might call the <a href=\"wizard-name-generator.html\">wizard</a> \"Talks-Too-Much\" and mean it as a simple fact.</li><li><strong>Change the name when it's earned.</strong> After a great deed, the tribe, or the lizardfolk themselves, may give a new descriptive name.</li><li><strong>Keep it practical.</strong> A lizardfolk named Frog-Eater isn't embarrassed. That's just what they eat.</li></ul>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "How do lizardfolk names work in D&D?",
        "answer": "In Volo's Guide to Monsters, lizardfolk names come from Draconic and are simple descriptions given by the tribe, based on a notable deed or trait."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does my lizardfolk's Draconic name mean?",
        "answer": "The Draconic-style names here are invented to sound right rather than real Draconic words, so you can decide. Use the Both format to pair each one with a descriptive name."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can a lizardfolk change their name?",
        "answer": "Yes. Because the name describes something they've done or are known for, a lizardfolk may earn a new one after a more notable deed."
      }
    ]
  }
